WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.820
European Orbiter detects hydrogen chloride above Martian fissures, hinting at lingering volcanic heat beneath the surface. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 244. After that, a Canadian spacewalk that finished the station's solar arrays, swifts fall over Indonesia, and a cosmic spotlight on Enceladus. Alba Mons, on Mars, showed fresh narrow fissures hundreds of meters long in images from the European Space Agency's exomars Trace Gas Orbiter, with color changes matching

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.200
deposits left by gas seeping out of the crust.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.860
The hydrogen chloride turned up in early northern summer, not in the seasonal rhythm that usually

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.900
follows dust and water, and an international team led by Alexander Trokomovsky and co-lead

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.120
author Daniel Mej, pulled the detections from the atmospheric chemistry suite infrared spectrometers,

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.360
and the CASIS camera.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.800
The team examined every chemical pathway they could identify in the air, ruled those cycles

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.700
miles out and tied the seapage to a magma chamber sitting quite under the crust.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.700
The stain around the cracks looks like the deposits that form around fumaroles on Earth,

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.880
and the orbiter caught only a handful of molecules while it kept watch over the entire Martian

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.400
atmosphere from orbit.

00:01:13.345 --> 00:01:18.265
Canadian astronaut Joshua Kutrick and a Neil Menon of the National Aeronautics and Space

00:01:18.265 --> 00:01:24.645
Administration, or NASA, in coverage from space.com, installed the eighth rollout solar array,

00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:31.445
the final one on the International Space Station or ISS. The pair closed a five-year power upgrade

00:01:31.445 --> 00:01:37.725
that lifts the station from 160 kilowatts to approximately 215 on a spacewalk of roughly

00:01:37.725 --> 00:01:40.985
seven hours that began at 8.30 in the morning Eastern time.

00:01:41.705 --> 00:01:47.505
Kutrick became the fifth Canadian to go outside on October 29th when the same duo fitted seven of

00:01:47.505 --> 00:01:53.445
the eight arrays and this latest excursion was the 100th United States Spacewalk stage from the station

00:01:53.445 --> 00:01:56.345
and the first by a Canadian since 2019.

00:01:57.285 --> 00:02:01.825
Kutrick and Menon are sharing the outposts with five other crew members from NASA, the

00:02:01.825 --> 00:02:04.245
Canadian Space Agency, and Ross Cosmos.

00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:12.547
The Neil Garell Swift Observatory, a 1,450kg spacecraft, came down at 739 in the morning

00:02:12.547 --> 00:02:19.047
eastern time over northern Sumatra, according to Cazinform, ending 22 years of gamma-ray burst

00:02:19.047 --> 00:02:22.287
and supernova-watching after an attempted orbit boost failed.

00:02:22.287 --> 00:02:26.987
world. Most of the vehicle was expected to burn up, with the chance of debris causing

00:02:26.987 --> 00:02:34.607
injury put at 1 in 2,100, and its last observation was logged on October 20. Swift left Earth in

00:02:34.607 --> 00:02:40.887
November 2004 for a two-year plan, then kept working nearly two decades past that mark, and

00:02:40.887 --> 00:02:47.587
in 2018 it was renamed for principal investigator Neil Garell's. Sean Domegal Goldman, director

00:02:47.587 --> 00:02:52.727
of NASA's Astrophysics Division said the mission delivered an incredible return on investment,

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:57.867
and the fall followed a private spacecraft try in July that lost control and could not

00:02:57.867 --> 00:02:58.627
raise the orbit.

00:02:59.627 --> 00:03:06.127
The crewed X-70 fractal unveiled by NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman at the UP Summit at Kennedy Space

00:03:06.127 --> 00:03:12.147
Center in a report from the aviationist will test rapidly reconfigurable flight control technology.

00:03:12.147 --> 00:03:20.927
It is built to cruise at Mach 0.75, climb to 45,000 feet, and cover more than 1,500 nautical miles,

00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:27.607
with a takeoff weight of 10,000 pounds and room for up to 2,000 pounds of internal or external payloads.

00:03:28.047 --> 00:03:34.167
The modular airframe is meant for quick swaps of those payloads, and a first flight is expected within two years.

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:38.927
Los Angeles, Laurie Grindel, director of the Aeronautics Division, said the X-70 joins

00:03:38.927 --> 00:03:44.187
a storied legacy of X-planes that advanced American aviation, and the aircraft is supposed

00:03:44.187 --> 00:03:49.447
to explore maneuverability never before shown in crude flight, with Isaacman adding that

00:03:49.447 --> 00:03:52.787
the science fiction future children imagined is ours to build.

00:03:53.621 --> 00:04:00.341
Three companies in an aviation week account received NASA contracts worth up to $1.065 billion

00:04:00.341 --> 00:04:07.601
dollars to build commercial KABBAND relay services. With AstraZan is taking up to $264.3 million,

00:04:08.421 --> 00:04:15.481
global enterprises solutions up to $340.4 million, and via SAT up to $460.9 million.

00:04:15.961 --> 00:04:19.961
The work is meant to replace the aging, tracking, and data relay satellite fleet,

00:04:20.341 --> 00:04:25.481
and each firm fixed price award lets NASA move from concept development to an orbital flight

00:04:25.481 --> 00:04:31.161
demonstration. The first performance window runs through May 20, 2027, with further

00:04:31.161 --> 00:04:37.381
options through 2029, matching the agency's plan to retire that network and buy commercial links

00:04:37.381 --> 00:04:43.461
instead. Seven tracking and data relay satellite spacecraft remain active, supporting the station

00:04:43.461 --> 00:04:49.126
and Hubble until they are taken out of service. Alex McDonald, NASA's former chief economist,

00:04:49.406 --> 00:04:55.426
was named President of the Canadian Space Agency, in reporting from SpaceQ, succeeding Lisa Campbell

00:04:55.426 --> 00:04:57.846
and Acting President John Claude Piedbooth.

00:04:58.066 --> 00:05:02.966
He has pushed for faster procurement and a turn toward lunar surface robotics after NASA

00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:08.526
paused the lunar gateway, and he spent 17 years at the agency, beginning at Ames Research

00:05:08.526 --> 00:05:13.026
Center and later serving as founding program executive of the Emerging Space Office.

00:05:13.726 --> 00:05:19.706
McDonald wrote the 2017 book, The Long Space Age, has argued that Canada should buy rides

00:05:19.706 --> 00:05:26.906
on NASA commercial lunar payload services landers in 2027 and 2028, and has flagged openings

00:05:26.906 --> 00:05:31.746
in space nuclear power, medical systems, and food production for Canadian firms.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:37.750
Deputy Chief Designer Wang Chiang, speaking for the China Aerospace Science and Technology

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:43.730
Corporation on November 3rd, as Universe today reported, laid out China's crude moon landing

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:44.410
architecture.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:49.750
The plan calls for new launch vehicles and a dedicated lunar lander to put astronauts on

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:57.391
the surface. T-O-I-561-B in findings carried by Incor keeps an atmosphere even though a global

00:05:57.391 --> 00:06:02.651
magma ocean covers the planet. The result sits against models that expect air to strip away

00:06:02.651 --> 00:06:07.911
quickly on worlds like this, and the planet orbits its star so tightly that the ground is a sea of

00:06:07.911 --> 00:06:16.253
molten rock. Enceladus in a NASA image from November 1st 2009 hangs as a 500 kilometer icy moon in

00:06:16.253 --> 00:06:22.693
silhouette, with south polar plumes feeding Saturn's e-ring. Pandora, 80 km across, shows

00:06:22.693 --> 00:06:27.993
up faintly beyond the rings, lit by Saturn light, and the ice reflects as brightly as fresh

00:06:27.993 --> 00:06:34.093
snow. Cassie Nieve first found those jets in 2005, and they are likely tied to water under

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:39.413
the ice shell. Stand in Saturn's shadow, look toward the sun, and put that small ball of

00:06:39.413 --> 00:06:44.293
ice between you and the light, and the geometry turns the moon into a dark disc rimmed with

00:06:44.293 --> 00:06:49.773
sun, so anything leaving the surface draws bright streaks on black. The south polar plumes

00:06:49.773 --> 00:06:54.553
stretch hundreds of kilometers into vacuum, and load particles straight into the outermost

00:06:54.553 --> 00:06:59.473
ring, while the same frame catches Pandora glowing from light bouncing off the planet's

00:06:59.473 --> 00:07:04.673
icy rings. A reminder that a small moon can be lit by its parent when the sun sits behind

00:07:04.673 --> 00:07:10.913
both. Cassini itself was moving at several kilometers per second, yet a long exposure still

00:07:10.913 --> 00:07:15.593
frows the jets, because the grains were climbing outward at roughly the escape speed from

00:07:15.593 --> 00:07:17.073
Enceladus' weak gravity.

00:07:17.753 --> 00:07:22.473
Ice that does not make it out falls back as a constant rain of tiny grains, which is

00:07:22.473 --> 00:07:27.173
why the ground looks freshly snow-covered, and sunlight alone could not drive those jets.

00:07:27.753 --> 00:07:33.013
The source that fits is liquid water under the shell, kept warm by tidal flexing from Saturn.

00:07:33.493 --> 00:07:38.953
The 2009 backlit view still shows the venting as ongoing and pinned to the south pole,

00:07:38.953 --> 00:07:44.553
and what remains unsettled is why the material comes out in separate jets instead of a uniform sheet,

00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:49.613
and whether the fractures that feed them open and close as Enceladus swings around Saturn.

00:07:50.373 --> 00:07:55.833
Craft with mass spectrometers and sharper images could sample the plume and map those cracks in three

00:07:55.833 --> 00:08:01.593
dimensions, until then the silhouette is the most direct evidence that this moon is venting its interior

00:08:01.593 --> 00:08:08.747
ocean into space. The outer space craft in an aviation week report lost its communications link during

00:08:08.747 --> 00:08:14.247
